Chapter: GuardRailCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter GuardRailGold

 Grade the GuardRail port against wasm/guard_rail.zig.

 TWO SEAMS, because guard_rail.zig is deliberately two halves and they fail in
 different ways.

 emit is seam 2 on a structure: it COLLECTS polys rather than drawing them, so the
 gold is read straight off the store -- the poly count per case, every colour, the
 mean forward depth each poly sorts by, and all twelve corner coordinates. The
 DEPTH IS GRADED, not merely used: it is guard_rail's whole contribution to
 render's unified back-to-front pass, and a rail sorted wrong is a rail a tree
 paints over, which is the exact bug the collect-then-sort design exists to
 prevent. A wrong depth would be invisible to a check that only looked at pixels.

 drawPoly is seam 3: it writes paint's buffer, so the gold is the words the game
 actually put on the wire, decoded back into tags, point counts and coordinates.

 STRUCTURE EXACTLY, COORDINATES WITH TOLERANCE, as everywhere in this port. The
 poly counts, colours, tags and point counts take no tolerance at all. World
 coordinates are metres and take the relative gate; screen coordinates take the
 mixed pixel gate, for the cancellation reason Grade sets out.

 THE CASES ARE BRANCHES, NOT COVERAGE:

   corner  a realistic outer-corner path -- run-up, both legs into and out of the
           apex, run-out. Six bars and seven posts.
   dupe    a path whose third point REPEATS its first, so the middle post's two
           neighbours coincide, its run length is exactly zero, and the fallback
           direction fires. That test is bitwise in the zig and bitwise here; a
           4-ULP tolerance would fire it on merely-close points and swing a post.
   near    a path running from behind the eye to well ahead. Five quads sit wholly
           behind the near plane and must be DROPPED -- twenty-nine polys are
           emitted across the four cases and only twenty-four reach the wire, and
           that difference is the assertion.
   single  one point. Fewer than two has no run direction, so emit raises nothing.

 A rail quad is vertical: both of its bottom corners share a forward with the two
 above them. So the near plane always cuts it 0, 2 or 4 vertices in, never 3, and
 every surviving poly still has four points. That is why rail-counts is uniformly
 4 and is a property being asserted, not a coincidence being tolerated.

 We say:

Section: The Paths

  path-corner : List RiderPt
  path-corner = [RiderPt { right = 6.0, forward = 44.0 }, RiderPt { right = 6.0, forward = 34.0 }, RiderPt { right = 6.0, forward = 24.0 }, RiderPt { right = 7.4, forward = 17.5 }, RiderPt { right = 11.0, forward = 13.0 }, RiderPt { right = 17.0, forward = 11.4 }, RiderPt { right = 27.0, forward = 11.4 }]

  path-dupe : List RiderPt
  path-dupe = [RiderPt { right = 0.0 - 4.0, forward = 20.0 }, RiderPt { right = 0.0 - 4.0, forward = 12.0 }, RiderPt { right = 0.0 - 4.0, forward = 20.0 }, RiderPt { right = 0.0 - 4.5, forward = 6.0 }]

  path-near : List RiderPt
  path-near = [RiderPt { right = 3.0, forward = 0.0 - 12.0 }, RiderPt { right = 3.0, forward = 0.0 - 4.0 }, RiderPt { right = 3.0, forward = 0.2 }, RiderPt { right = 3.2, forward = 6.0 }, RiderPt { right = 3.6, forward = 30.0 }]

  path-single : List RiderPt
  path-single = [RiderPt { right = 2.0, forward = 9.0 }]

Section: The Stores

  store-corner : List RailPoly
  store-corner = rail-emit path-corner

  store-dupe : List RailPoly
  store-dupe = rail-emit path-dupe

  store-near : List RailPoly
  store-near = rail-emit path-near

  store-single : List RailPoly
  store-single = rail-emit path-single

  all-polys : List RailPoly
  all-polys = store-corner & store-dupe & store-near & store-single

 The per-case poly count, in the probe's case order. Six bars plus seven posts,
 three plus four, four plus five, and nothing.

  poly-counts : List Integer
  poly-counts = [list-length store-corner, list-length store-dupe, list-length store-near, list-length store-single]

Section: The Store Streams

  poly-colors : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Integer
  poly-colors (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).color] & poly-colors ps (i + 1)

  poly-fwds : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Real
  poly-fwds (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else [(list-at ps i).fwd] & poly-fwds ps (i + 1)

 Every corner of every poly, right then forward then height, in vertex order --
 the same walk the probe makes over the store's fixed array.

  flatten-vec3 : List Vec3, Integer -> List Real
  flatten-vec3 (vs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length vs then []
    else let v = list-at vs i
    in [v.right, v.forward, v.height] & flatten-vec3 vs (i + 1)

  poly-verts : List RailPoly, Integer -> List Real
  poly-verts (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then []
    else (flatten-vec3 (list-at ps i).v 0) & poly-verts ps (i + 1)

Section: The Wire Streams

 The store drawn in STORE ORDER, which is what the probe does. Render would sort
 first; the sort is render's and is not what is under test here.

  wire : List DrawCmd
  wire = (rail-draw-all store-corner focal camera-w 0) & (rail-draw-all store-dupe focal camera-w 0) & (rail-draw-all store-near focal camera-w 0) & (rail-draw-all store-single focal camera-w 0)

  cmd-tags : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  cmd-tags (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & cmd-tags cs (i + 1)

  cmd-counts : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  cmd-counts (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [list-length (list-at cs i).pts / 2] & cmd-counts cs (i + 1)

  cmd-coords : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  cmd-coords (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).pts & cmd-coords cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "rail-polys  " poly-counts g-rail-polys)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "rail-colors " (poly-colors all-polys 0) g-rail-colors)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "rail-fwd    " (poly-fwds all-polys 0) g-rail-fwd 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "rail-verts  " (poly-verts all-polys 0) g-rail-verts 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "rail-tags   " (cmd-tags wire 0) g-rail-tags)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "rail-counts " (cmd-counts wire 0) g-rail-counts)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "rail-coords " (cmd-coords wire 0) g-rail-coords 0.001 0.000001)
  end
